  "evidence": "A: Massive care commitments — NHS: end privatisation, rapid GP access, universal NHS dentistry, major funding increases (£8bn year one rising to £28bn by 2030), mental health within 28 days, counsellor in every school; Social care: free personal care with £20bn/year, fair pay for carers, disability benefits +5%, improvements to children’s social care; Welfare: raise Universal Credit by £40/week, scrap two‑child cap, higher Carer’s Allowance. B: Strong authority measures — mandatory National Service at 18; 8,000 more neighbourhood police, facial recognition, tougher sentences, four new prisons; borders: monthly Rwanda removals, rewrite asylum treaties, falling legal migration cap, higher visa fees/health checks; defence to 2.5% of GDP; some care via NHS funding above inflation and staff recruitment; welfare tightened with tougher sanctions and stricter capability rules.",
  "reasoning": "Because care is the top priority in the profile, A aligns better by centring expansive health, social care and anti‑poverty measures that directly reduce harm and support nurturance. While the profile allows some authority (0.25), B leans heavily into authority, policing and border control and pairs welfare with sanctions, so its overall fit to the care‑led profile is weaker than A’s.",
  "confidence": "medium",
  "winner": "A"
}
